<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">André Gide</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1869">1869</a>–<a href="/years/1951">1951</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as André Paul Guillaume Gide</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">André Gide was a French author, born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> in 1869 into a middle-class Protestant family, who wrote more than 50 books and received the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nobel-prize">Nobel Prize</a> in Literature in 1947. His first novel, The Notebooks of André Walter, appeared in 1891, and his fiction and autobiographical works explored the conflict between Protestant austerity and his sexuality. His journey through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-equatorial-africa">French Equatorial Africa</a> in 1926–1927 produced Travels in the Congo, which likened French companies' exploitation of African workers to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> and fed the anti-colonial movement in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. Sympathetic to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/communism">Communism</a> in the early 1930s, he turned against Stalin after visiting the USSR in 1936. He died in Paris in 1951.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/andre-gide.jpg" alt="André Gide" width="330" height="352" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: unknow. uploader Claudio Elias · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:André_Gide.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
